# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5.memetic-wikitext" "A lar: address ends before the doubled mark that closes the span it stands in"

# A lar: path took every character but whitespace, a quote, a backtick, `>`, `]`, `?` and `#`, so it ate
# the `//` that closes an italic run (and `__`, `^^`, `,,`, `~~` alike): `//see lar:///a.b.c/d//` left
# the italic open to the end of the line. A path segment never doubles those marks, so the address now
# ends before one.

//see lar:///a.b.c/d// then more
#                   ^^ punctuation.definition.markup.end.italic.tiddlywiki5
#                      ^^^^ - markup.italic.tiddlywiki5

__see lar:///a.b.c/d__ then more
#                      ^^^^ - markup.underline.tiddlywiki5

^^see lar:///a.b.c/d^^ then more
#                      ^^^^ - markup.superscript.tiddlywiki5

,,see lar:///a.b.c/d,, then more
#                      ^^^^ - markup.subscript.tiddlywiki5

~~see lar:///a.b.c/d~~ then more
#                      ^^^^ - markup.strikethrough.tiddlywiki5

# CONTROL: a single mark inside a path stays path text.

x lar:///a.b.c/d_e/f^g y
#              ^^^ variable.other.constant.path.lar.memetic-wikitext

# INSIDE A VALUE NO SPAN OPENS, so nothing there needs a doubled mark left to it: a quoted or
# ticked value reads its whole path, snake__case and all.

<<m a="lar:///a.b.c/snake__case">>
#                   ^^^^^^^^^^^ variable.other.constant.path.lar.memetic-wikitext

<<m a=`lar:///a.b.c/x//y~~z`>>
#                   ^^^^^^^ variable.other.constant.path.lar.memetic-wikitext
